```java
if (logger.isWarnEnabled()) {
    logger.warn("Caught exception while allowing TestExecutionListener [" + testExecutionListener +
            "] to process 'after class' callback for test class [" + testClass + "]", ex);
    if (afterTestClassException == null) {
        afterTestClassException = ex;
    }
}
```
Reason:
The assignment of the `afterTestClassException` variable is placed inside the conditional block that checks if logging is enabled. If logging is not enabled, the exception will not be recorded, resulting in after-class exceptions going untracked.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
if (logger.isWarnEnabled()) {
    logger.warn("Caught exception while allowing TestExecutionListener [" + testExecutionListener +
            "] to process 'after class' callback for test class [" + testClass + "]", ex);
}
if (afterTestClassException == null) {
    afterTestClassException = ex;
}
```
Reason:
By moving the assignment of `afterTestClassException` outside of the logging conditional block, the exception will be recorded regardless of whether logging is enabled or not, ensuring that after-class exceptions are always tracked.

